  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227428/assets/f498fdcd8c6be58b8d1c1e6e4c3d835799a8c571cdd9125f7e7555307c94a6d1.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty $\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=1$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's compute the limits in question.\n\nWe see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $3$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $3$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is true.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true.",
